; `convert` pushes the CSV file onto the session's parse-context stack.
; It used to leave that context on the stack, so in a REPL or --script
; session every later reader of the current context saw the CSV's
; directory as the working directory: a relative `--import` path then
; resolved under test/regress/ instead of the source root and failed with
; "Python failed to import".  The context must be popped whether the
; conversion succeeds or throws.

account Assets:Checking
account Expenses:Unknown

; Success path: two conversions in one script, then a relative --import.
test --script test/regress/convert-parse-context.dat
2024/01/05 * Grocer
    Expenses:Unknown                       12.50 USD
    Assets:Checking

2024/01/06 * Bakery
    Expenses:Unknown                        4.25 USD
    Assets:Checking
2024/01/05 * Grocer
    Expenses:Unknown                       12.50 USD
    Assets:Checking

2024/01/06 * Bakery
    Expenses:Unknown                        4.25 USD
    Assets:Checking
          -33.50 USD  Assets:Checking
           33.50 USD  Expenses:Unknown
--------------------
                   0
end test

; Error path: a conversion that throws, then a relative --import.  The
; REPL (stdin) keeps going after an error, unlike --script; only the
; error lines are kept so the banner does not enter the expectation.
test -f $FILE < test/regress/convert-parse-context-error.dat 2>&1 | grep '^Error'
Error: No quantity specified for amount
end test

; A CSV that cannot be opened throws from the push itself: nothing was
; pushed, so nothing may be popped (popping would remove the session's
; own base context).
test -f $FILE < test/regress/convert-parse-context-missing.dat 2>&1 | grep '^Error' | sed 's|".*/|"|'
Error: Cannot read journal file "convert-parse-context-missing.csv"
end test
